Andrew Campling said:

@EKR useful to have, assuming that a recharter expands the scope of the wg

I don't really agree, no

Adrian Farrel said:

@EKR Hmmm, maybe that was a comment about chartering the WG?

The charter says "The WG will consider DNS and mDNS as mechanisms upon which to build a discovery protocol.". It should consider them and then reject htem

Andrew Campling said:

@EKR or we could decide to use DNS.  ISTM we should think v.carefully before rejecting and building new thing just because we can.

I'm not talking about building a new thing. I'm talking about putting it in /.well-known, which some of these drafts already do.

Éric Vyncke said:

@Erik: we should also support endpoint not supporting HTTPS

[citation needed]

James Mozley said:

@EKR when I checked the a2a spec protocols other than https were possible

Yes, but as a practical matter those servers can just have a simple HTTPS server. They're going to need to have a WebPKI cert in any case, because that's how endpoints on the Internet are authenticated.

James Mozley said:

@EKR when I checked the a2a spec protocols other than https were possible

Others are used locally on the same system but I guess you need http over the internet.

Éric Vyncke said in #dawn > ietf-127:

@ekr most probably as I cannot imagine doing this w/o. Of course, "universal deployment of DNSSEC..."

So we're predicating the success of this protocol on a protocol which, I think could most generously be described as having yet to succeed.

@ekr 

Right. I think the local names problem is basically a research problem.

Agreed -- that is my point above that how do to local names is going to be the unsolved "hard problem" here, but solving it depends on something non-existent and out-of-charter.

Andrew Campling said:

@EKR or use DNSSEC?

You mean use a protocol which has trivial levels of deployment as opposed to a protocol which has essentially universal deployment?

Andrew Campling said:

@EKR trivial is a stretch

No, it's really not.  Last time I checked it was single digits

Ramachandra Seethiraju said:

@Benjamin Schwartz and for those interested in the DNS sizing and efficiency questions rasied today - we evaluated agent discovery metadata using real-world API and MCP datasets including DNS message size constraints and lookup overhead here - https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.02314

Thanks.  It looks like this is really designed for enumerating MCP tool indexes, not "agents".  A large company could easily have hundreds of thousands of "agents", and many thousands of MCP tools, but only a small number of MCP tool indexes.  ISTM that we are essentially talking about indexes of indexes, but the terminology is very confusing to me.
